"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[18456],{218456:e=>{e.exports="# Redshift Dialect — Cross-Cutting Quirks & Behavioral Drift\n\nApplies to Amazon Redshift (provisioned clusters and Redshift Serverless). Loaded eagerly before any pipeline step. Covers patterns that span all stages. Per-feature mappings live in the topic files — fetch them during conversion via the Construct Routing Table below.\n\n## Cross-cutting syntax quirks\n\n### Dollar quoting (`$$ ... $$`)\n\nRedshift wraps function/procedure bodies in `$$ ... $$` (or named `$tag$ ... $tag$`). Translation depends on the body language: strip `$$` for SQL UDFs and PL/pgSQL procedures; **keep `$$` for `LANGUAGE PYTHON` UDFs**. See `04-plpgsql.md` for the full rules and examples.\n\n### Double-quoted identifiers → backticks\n\nRedshift (PostgreSQL) uses `\"quoted identifier\"`; DBSQL uses backticks:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nSELECT \"order id\" FROM \"sales schema\".\"orders\"\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT `order id` FROM sales_schema.orders\n```\n\nDBSQL also accepts double quotes in ANSI mode, but backticks are the standard.\n\n### `::` type cast — supported natively\n\nRedshift's PostgreSQL-style `expr::type` cast is **a native DBSQL operator** with identical semantics. Leave unchanged unless rewriting for clarity. See `02-types-and-coercion.md` for type-mapping details.\n\n### Maintenance and session-tuning commands — mostly remove\n\nRedshift `VACUUM`, `ANALYZE [COMPRESSION]`, and `SET` parameters (`search_path`, `wlm_query_slot_count`, `statement_timeout`, `enable_case_sensitive_identifier`, `mv_enable_aqmv_for_session`, etc.) are routine maintenance/tuning calls that have no DBSQL analogue and should be removed. Two notable exceptions: `SET search_path` → `USE SCHEMA catalog.schema`, and `ANALYZE <table>` invoked explicitly before a planner-sensitive query → `ANALYZE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t COMPUTE STATISTICS`. **Do not substitute DBSQL `VACUUM`** — it cleans up time-travel files, a different concept that can break time-travel queries. See `01-schema-and-storage.md` for the full mapping.\n\n## Behavioral drift\n\nFunctions that exist in both Redshift and DBSQL but behave differently — highest-risk patterns because they pass syntax validation and silently produce wrong results.\n\n### Implicit string↔numeric casting — Redshift is more permissive\n\nRedshift silently coerces between numbers and numeric strings in comparisons and arithmetic. DBSQL (in ANSI mode, the default) raises an error. Scan for these patterns:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift (works)\nWHERE order_id = '12345'     -- integer column compared to string literal\nSELECT '100' + 1              -- string + int = 101\n\n-- DBSQL (errors — add explicit cast)\nWHERE order_id = CAST('12345' AS INT)\nSELECT CAST('100' AS INT) + 1\n```\n\nAdd explicit `CAST` or `TRY_CAST` at every cross-type comparison or arithmetic site.\n\n### Division by zero\n\n| Redshift                           | DBSQL                                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Returns `NULL` silently by default | Raises an error, unless `ansi.enabled=false` or wrapped in `TRY_DIVIDE` |\n\n**Do not blanket-substitute `/` with `try_divide`.** Only rewrite when the source code relied on the NULL-on-zero behavior (usually seen as `SELECT a / NULLIF(b, 0)` already, or inside `CASE WHEN b = 0 THEN NULL ELSE a/b END`). If the source code expected divide-by-zero to produce a row, preserve that intent explicitly:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL, NULL on zero denominator\nSELECT a / NULLIF(b, 0)\n-- or\nSELECT TRY_DIVIDE(a, b)\n```\n\n### NULL ordering — defaults drift in both directions\n\nRedshift treats NULLs as the **highest** value by default:\n\n- `ORDER BY col ASC` → NULLs **last**\n- `ORDER BY col DESC` → NULLs **first**\n\nDBSQL treats NULLs as the **lowest** value by default (per [ORDER BY docs](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-orderby)):\n\n- `ORDER BY col ASC` → NULLs **first**\n- `ORDER BY col DESC` → NULLs **last**\n\nThis means drift is always present in both directions when the source has an unqualified `ORDER BY` that touches a nullable column.\n\n| Direction           | Redshift        | DBSQL default   | Fix                                 |\n| ------------------- | --------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `ORDER BY col ASC`  | NULLs **last**  | NULLs **first** | Add `NULLS LAST` to match Redshift  |\n| `ORDER BY col DESC` | NULLs **first** | NULLs **last**  | Add `NULLS FIRST` to match Redshift |\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC;   -- NULLs appear first\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — NULLs appear last by default on DESC)\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC;\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT — matches Redshift semantics)\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS FIRST;\n```\n\n**Scope**: only apply this fix where NULL position affects visible results — top-level `ORDER BY` and `OVER (... ORDER BY ...)` in window functions where the window value depends on row order (e.g. `ROW_NUMBER`, running totals). Ordering inside subqueries used only for filtering or non-order-sensitive aggregations does not need `NULLS` qualifiers.\n\nIf the source uses explicit `NULLS FIRST` / `NULLS LAST` qualifiers, carry them through verbatim — they are supported in DBSQL.\n\n### Case sensitivity\n\nRedshift identifiers are case-insensitive by default (unless `enable_case_sensitive_identifier` is set). String comparison respects column collation. DBSQL identifiers are case-insensitive in standard mode; string comparison is **case-sensitive by default**.\n\nIf the source relies on case-insensitive string equality in WHERE/JOIN, flag and suggest:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL — case-insensitive comparison\nWHERE name COLLATE UTF8_LCASE = 'john'\n-- or at column level\nCREATE TABLE t (name STRING COLLATE UTF8_LCASE)\n```\n\n> For DBSQL collation syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/collation.md`.\n\n## Safe functions — no translation needed\n\nIdentical in both Redshift and DBSQL — leave unchanged.\n\n**String:** `SUBSTRING`, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `TRIM`, `LTRIM`, `RTRIM`, `REPLACE`, `REVERSE`, `UPPER`, `LOWER`, `INITCAP`, `LPAD`, `RPAD`, `ASCII`, `CHR`, `CHARINDEX`, `POSITION`, `LEN`/`LENGTH`, `OCTET_LENGTH`, `SPLIT_PART`, `REGEXP_REPLACE`. (Note: Redshift `STRPOS` → DBSQL `INSTR` — see `07-functions.md`.)\n\n**Math:** `ABS`, `FLOOR`, `CEIL`, `CEILING`, `ROUND`, `SQRT`, `POWER`, `SIGN`, `RANDOM`, `LOG`, `LOG10`, `LN`, `EXP`, `PI`, `MOD`, `DEGREES`, `RADIANS`, `SIN`, `COS`, `TAN`, `ASIN`, `ACOS`, `ATAN`, `ATAN2`, `CBRT`.\n\n**Aggregate:** `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `STDDEV`, `STDDEV_SAMP`, `STDDEV_POP`, `VARIANCE`, `VAR_SAMP`, `VAR_POP`, `REGR_SLOPE`, `REGR_INTERCEPT`, `REGR_R2`.\n\n**Window:** `ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `DENSE_RANK`, `NTILE`, `PERCENT_RANK`, `CUME_DIST`, `LAG`, `LEAD`, `FIRST_VALUE`, `LAST_VALUE`, `NTH_VALUE` (with `IGNORE NULLS` and full frame clauses).\n\n**Date/time:** `LAST_DAY`, `DATE_TRUNC`, `EXTRACT`, `NEXT_DAY`, `MONTHS_BETWEEN`, `MAKE_INTERVAL`.\n\n**Hash:** `MD5`, `SHA1` (use `SHA2(str, 256)` for `SHA256`, `SHA2(str, 512)` for `SHA512` — see `07-functions.md`).\n\n**Other:** `GREATEST`, `LEAST`, `COALESCE`, `NVL`, `NVL2`, `NULLIF`, `DECODE`, `UNION`, `EXCEPT`/`MINUS`, `INTERSECT`.\n\n---\n\n## Construct Routing Table\n\nDuring the Plan step, scan the live source SQL for these patterns. Load the matching reference files via `readSkillFile` using the full paths shown. When adding a new reference file, update this table and `references/00-index.md` (human reference).\n\n| Pattern in source SQL                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Reference file (full path for `readSkillFile`)                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER TABLE`, `DISTSTYLE`, `DISTKEY`, `SORTKEY`, `ENCODE`, `IDENTITY`, `CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE`, `COMMENT ON`, `VACUUM`, `ANALYZE`, `SET search_path`, `SET wlm_*`, `SET statement_timeout`, cluster tuning    | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/01-schema-and-storage.md`    |\n| `VARCHAR`, `CHAR`, `SUPER`, `HLLSKETCH`, `GEOMETRY`, `TIMESTAMPTZ`, `INTERVAL`, `::` casts, `CAST` / `CONVERT` / `TO_NUMBER`                                                                                                   | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/02-types-and-coercion.md`    |\n| `LISTAGG`, `MEDIAN`, `APPROXIMATE COUNT`, `QUALIFY`, `JSON_EXTRACT_PATH_TEXT`, `UNION ALL`, `EXCEPT`, `MERGE` predicate forms, `UPDATE ... FROM`, `DELETE ... USING`                                                           | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/03-query-and-dml.md`         |\n| `CREATE [OR REPLACE] PROCEDURE`, `CREATE [OR REPLACE] FUNCTION`, `$$ ... $$`, `DECLARE`, `RAISE`, `plpgsql`, `plpythonu`, `IF`/`ELSIF`/`ELSE`, `LOOP`, `FOR`, `WHILE`, `EXIT`, `CONTINUE`, `EXECUTE`, `CURSOR`, error handling | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/04-plpgsql.md`               |\n| `CREATE TEMP TABLE`, `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE`, recursive CTEs                                                                                                                                                                  | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/05-temp-and-ctes.md`         |\n| `COPY ... FROM 's3://'`, `UNLOAD`, materialized views with `AUTO REFRESH`, scheduled pipelines, `BEGIN`, `COMMIT`, `ROLLBACK`, `LOCK TABLE`, isolation levels                                                                  | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/06-etl-and-data-movement.md` |\n| `GETDATE`, `SYSDATE`, `DATEADD`, `DATEDIFF`, `CONVERT_TIMEZONE`, `TRUNC(date)`, `CHARINDEX`, `POSITION`, `STRPOS`, `NVL2`, `DECODE`, `CURRENT_USER`, `CURRENT_DATABASE`, `CURRENT_SCHEMA`, leader-node-only functions          | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/07-functions.md`             |\n| `$$` quoting, double-quoted identifiers, `::` casts, unqualified `ORDER BY` on nullable columns, implicit string↔numeric casts, `/` divide-by-zero                                                                             | Covered in this file — no additional load needed                                   |\n\n**Dynamic SQL — procedure is untranslatable**: If a procedure builds a string in a variable and executes it via `EXECUTE var_sql`, the whole procedure body cannot be mechanically translated. Output a single TODO block:\n\n```sql\n-- TODO: procedure builds and executes dynamic SQL — manual rewrite required\n```\n\n---\n\n## Large-file Chunking\n\nRedshift has no explicit batch separator like T-SQL's `GO` or Oracle's `/`. When the source exceeds 300 lines, run `grepAsset` with this pattern to locate procedure/function definition boundaries:\n\n```\nCREATE\\s+(?:OR\\s+REPLACE\\s+)?(?:PROCEDURE|FUNCTION)\\s\n```\n\nUse each match as the start of an independent unit and process one procedure/function at a time. If no matches are found, process as a single unit.\n\n---\n\n## TODO Reference\n\n### Constructs that cannot be translated (valid TODO)\n\n| Redshift construct                                                         | Reason                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Python UDFs (`LANGUAGE plpythonu`) with non-stdlib imports or table return | Non-stdlib packages and table-return are not supported in the DBSQL Python UDF sandbox — port to PySpark UDF in a notebook. Stdlib-only scalar UDFs CAN be translated to `LANGUAGE PYTHON` — see `04-plpgsql.md` |\n| Lambda UDFs (`CREATE EXTERNAL FUNCTION ... LAMBDA`)                        | AWS Lambda integration — no DBSQL equivalent                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| Machine-learning functions (`CREATE MODEL`, `EXPLAIN MODEL`)               | Redshift ML — not available; rewrite with Databricks ML                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `STV_*` / `STL_*` / `SVV_*` system tables                                  | Redshift-specific introspection — no direct equivalent                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `UNLOAD` to S3                                                             | No one-liner; rewrite as `CREATE TABLE ... AS` or DataFrame write                                                                                                                                                |\n| Materialized views with `AUTO REFRESH`                                     | Use Delta Live Tables / Materialized Views; not a one-shot port                                                                                                                                                  |\n\n### Constructs that ARE supported (not valid TODO reasons)\n\n| Redshift construct                                         | DBSQL equivalent                                        | Reference file                |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Cursors                                                    | `DECLARE` / `FETCH` in SQL scripting (DBR 18.1+)        | `04-plpgsql.md`               |\n| Temp tables (`CREATE TEMP TABLE`)                          | `CREATE TEMP TABLE` (SQL warehouse) or CTE              | `05-temp-and-ctes.md`         |\n| PL/pgSQL procedures (`$$ ... $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql`)         | SQL scripting `BEGIN ... END` block with `LANGUAGE SQL` | `04-plpgsql.md`               |\n| Error handling (`RAISE`, `EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN`)        | `SIGNAL`, `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION`       | `04-plpgsql.md`               |\n| Complex control flow (`IF`/`ELSIF`/`ELSE`, `WHILE`, `FOR`) | DBSQL scripting equivalents                             | `04-plpgsql.md`               |\n| `COPY FROM 's3://...'`                                     | `COPY INTO ... FROM 's3://...' FILEFORMAT = ...`        | `06-etl-and-data-movement.md` |\n\n---\n\n## Validation Checklist\n\nAction table moved to `references/redshift/00-validation-patterns.md` — load that file via `readSkillFile` during the Validate step. It contains both the combined `grepAsset` regex and the pattern → action rows.\n"}}]);
